    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Example"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "code": "nums = [1, 3, 5, 6], target = 5  ->  2\nnums = [1, 3, 5, 6], target = 2  ->  1\nnums = [1, 3, 5, 6], target = 7  ->  4\nnums = [1, 3, 5, 6], target = 0  ->  0"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The idea: where binary search gives up"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Found or not, the answer is the **first index whose value is at least the target** — the lower bound. You can find it with the lower-bound template, but there's a neat fact about the classic loop too: when it ends without finding the target, `low` has stopped **exactly at the insert position**. Every value left of `low` is smaller than the target and every value right of `high` is bigger, and the loop ends with `low = high + 1`, so `low` is the first index past all the smaller values."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "1. Linear scan"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function searchInsert(nums, target) {\n  for (let i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {\n    if (nums[i] >= target) return i;   // the first value at least the target\n  }\n  return nums.length;                   // bigger than everything: insert at the end\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def search_insert(nums, target):\n    for i, value in enumerate(nums):\n        if value >= target:\n            return i                      # the first value at least the target\n    return len(nums)                      # bigger than everything: insert at the end"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "int searchInsert(int[] nums, int target) {\n    for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {\n        if (nums[i] >= target) return i;  // the first value at least the target\n    }\n    return nums.length;                   // bigger than everything: insert at the end\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "int searchInsert(vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n    for (int i = 0; i < (int)nums.size(); i++) {\n        if (nums[i] >= target) return i;  // the first value at least the target\n    }\n    return nums.size();                   // bigger than everything: insert at the end\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(n), space O(1).**"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "2. Classic binary search, then return low"
    },
    {
      "type": "simulator",
      "program": "search-insert",
      "caption": "When the target isn't found, watch where low stops: it's the insert position."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function searchInsert(nums, target) {\n  let low = 0;\n  let high = nums.length - 1;\n  while (low <= high) {\n    const mid = low + Math.floor((high - low) / 2);\n    if (nums[mid] === target) return mid;\n    if (nums[mid] < target) low = mid + 1;\n    else high = mid - 1;\n  }\n  return low;                           // left of low: all smaller; right of high: all bigger\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def search_insert(nums, target):\n    low, high = 0, len(nums) - 1\n    while low <= high:\n        mid = (low + high) // 2\n        if nums[mid] == target:\n            return mid\n        if nums[mid] < target:\n            low = mid + 1\n        else:\n            high = mid - 1\n    return low                            # left of low: all smaller; right of high: all bigger"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "int searchInsert(int[] nums, int target) {\n    int low = 0, high = nums.length - 1;\n    while (low <= high) {\n        int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;\n        if (nums[mid] == target) return mid;\n        if (nums[mid] < target) low = mid + 1;\n        else high = mid - 1;\n    }\n    return low;                           // left of low: all smaller; right of high: all bigger\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "int searchInsert(vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n    int low = 0, high = (int)nums.size() - 1;\n    while (low <= high) {\n        int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;\n        if (nums[mid] == target) return mid;\n        if (nums[mid] < target) low = mid + 1;\n        else high = mid - 1;\n    }\n    return low;                           // left of low: all smaller; right of high: all bigger\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(log n), space O(1).** The only change from Binary Search is the last line: `return low` instead of `return -1`."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "3. The lower-bound template"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function searchInsert(nums, target) {\n  let low = 0, high = nums.length;      // the answer can be nums.length\n  while (low < high) {\n    const mid = low + Math.floor((high - low) / 2);\n    if (nums[mid] < target) low = mid + 1;\n    else high = mid;\n  }\n  return low;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "from bisect import bisect_left\n\ndef search_insert(nums, target):\n    return bisect_left(nums, target)      # Python's built-in lower bound"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "int searchInsert(int[] nums, int target) {\n    int low = 0, high = nums.length;      // the answer can be nums.length\n    while (low < high) {\n        int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;\n        if (nums[mid] < target) low = mid + 1;\n        else high = mid;\n    }\n    return low;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "int searchInsert(vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n    return lower_bound(nums.begin(), nums.end(), target) - nums.begin();   // C++'s built-in lower bound\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(log n), space O(1).** In an interview, mention the library call, then write the loop yourself."
    },
